00:45:32.863 --> 00:45:34.509
but I did that a little
bit on that interview,

00:45:34.509 --> 00:45:37.830
just 'cause she was so closed off to-

00:45:37.830 --> 00:45:39.312
- Reality.
- Being aware

00:45:39.312 --> 00:45:41.362
of what she was doing and not looking at,

00:45:42.563 --> 00:45:44.070
so I got a little bit judgemental,

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:45.560
which I've never really done on my videos

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:47.370
but I kind did a little
bit of that on that,

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:49.710
just 'cause she's asking for it,

00:45:49.710 --> 00:45:52.470
she's so in denial of
anything that she was doing,

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:55.760
I mean she's got a kid,
she's addicted to drugs,

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:57.360
fentanyl I think it was.
- Yes.

00:45:58.220 --> 00:45:59.810
- And she's got a pimp that
she's been in love with

00:45:59.810 --> 00:46:03.912
for two weeks and he's
managing her money, 100% of it

00:46:03.912 --> 00:46:05.470
and it's like oh come on,

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:06.823
everything is wrong here.

00:46:08.572 --> 00:46:11.180
I think I said something,
kind of indicating

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:14.650
can you not look at this
and see it more clearly?

00:46:14.650 --> 00:46:16.093
But she can't.

00:46:17.331 --> 00:46:18.400
- I'd like to talk about the artistry,

00:46:18.400 --> 00:46:20.280
because I think we would
be remiss if we don't

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:22.530
acknowledge the artistry to what you do.

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:24.810
You have developed an aesthetic,

00:46:24.810 --> 00:46:27.790
the opening is we hear
street sounds very often,

00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:30.240
ambient street sounds,
then we hear a voice

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:32.240
of the person who's
about to be interviewed,

00:46:32.240 --> 00:46:36.370
then you dissolve into a
title, black and white,

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:38.610
white over black and
then it dissolves again

00:46:38.610 --> 00:46:40.650
and we have a color image
of somebody speaking

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:43.500
and then eventually at
some strategic point,

00:46:43.500 --> 00:46:45.890
you would then go to your
portrait that you've done.

00:46:45.890 --> 00:46:47.530
You typically will start at the top,

00:46:47.530 --> 00:46:51.280
with the face and you basically tilt down,

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:52.620
I presume you've taken the picture

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:54.060
with a large format camera.

00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:55.510
- Yep.
- And then you tilt down

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:57.400
so you can get the
clarity 'cause you can't

00:46:57.400 --> 00:46:59.770
do a horizontal plane, but it works,

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:02.420
and it's an aesthetic that
the viewer comes to expect.

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:04.300
Did that come innately to you,

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:06.100
or is it something premeditated?

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:07.830
- It's really just a terrible compromise,

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:12.030
'cause I'm shooting these
very vertical portraits,

00:47:12.030 --> 00:47:15.850
I had a nine by 13 inch
camera custom made,

00:47:15.850 --> 00:47:17.760
so it was basically to
accommodate the shape

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:19.920
of the human body, the dimensions,

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:21.570
the height versus the width.

00:47:21.570 --> 00:47:24.120
And there is no way on social media,

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:27.813
which is our new world now
to display a tall vertical.

00:47:29.140 --> 00:47:29.973
- Yes.

00:47:29.973 --> 00:47:32.170
- Instagram is basically square
and so is everything else

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:35.427
and YouTube is horizontal and
since I'm doing interviews now

00:47:35.427 --> 00:47:37.440
and videos, those are horizontal

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:41.870
so how do you fit a long,
almost extremely long vertical

00:47:41.870 --> 00:47:43.190
into a horizontal format.

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:44.543
- Right.

00:47:44.543 --> 00:47:45.376
- You just don't.

00:47:45.376 --> 00:47:46.980
Put it sideways, (chuckling)
so the best thing

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:49.240
I could come up with is
this terrible compromise

00:47:49.240 --> 00:47:51.760
where I pan down the portrait,

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:54.740
I cringe every time I do it,
but there is no other way.

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:56.060
- But I think it's a good effect,

00:47:56.060 --> 00:47:58.243
because very often, I mean
I'm not trying to be unkind

00:47:58.243 --> 00:47:59.950
I'm just being realistic as you are,

00:47:59.950 --> 00:48:02.080
very often you'll see significant details

00:48:02.080 --> 00:48:06.080
as you begin to tilt
down, you begin to notice

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:10.053
scabs and cuts and dirty toenails.

00:48:11.942 --> 00:48:13.243
- The concept behind all of this,

00:48:13.243 --> 00:48:17.530
is to have an inter shot
in such a large resolution

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:22.520
that my idea was to print
12 or 15 foot tall portraits

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:26.230
in a gigantic space and
then have a large TV monitor

00:48:26.230 --> 00:48:28.940
playing the video alongside
that giant portrait.

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:30.290
So the portrait is really the star,

00:48:30.290 --> 00:48:32.060
the portrait is what these
are really all about,

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:34.410
and the video is just a
supplement to the portrait,

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:36.930
but I think what people have
gotten used to on YouTube

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:38.237
is that the interview's everything

00:48:38.237 --> 00:48:41.560
and the portraits disposable,
you don't have to look at it,

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:43.587
I've seen people comment,
"what's that stupid portrait

00:48:43.587 --> 00:48:45.240
"in there for?"

00:48:45.240 --> 00:48:46.690
To me it's really about the portrait,

00:48:46.690 --> 00:48:49.510
and the video is just
kind like a backstory

00:48:49.510 --> 00:48:50.343
to the portrait.

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:54.860
- You did a self disclosure video,

00:48:54.860 --> 00:48:56.730
in fact you may have done
two, but I've certainly

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:59.770
seen one where you explain
what the whole premise

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:02.410
behind Soft White Underbelly is,

00:49:02.410 --> 00:49:04.560
I notice that you seemed
uncomfortable Mark,

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:06.710
if I pointed out, looking
at the camera directly

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:08.820
you would do it sporadically
but most of the time

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:10.450
your eyes would wander around your studio

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:11.966
and then you would-

00:49:11.966 --> 00:49:14.783
- Yeah I'm not a, I don't like
being in front of the camera,

00:49:14.783 --> 00:49:15.616
it's not my thing.

00:49:15.616 --> 00:49:17.830
- And why's that, why don't you?

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:20.840
- I have two daughters, one is like me,

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:22.247
really uncomfortable in front of a camera

00:49:22.247 --> 00:49:25.560
and the other one she's meant,

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:27.490
my mom was a model, my
ex-wife was a model,

00:49:27.490 --> 00:49:29.610
so she has it in her
blood and she's certainly

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:32.220
picked up those genes and she's just

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:34.390
amazing in front of a camera.

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:35.840
Like every frame is beautiful

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:38.090
and my other daughter
is the opposite of that

00:49:38.960 --> 00:49:41.200
she takes after me, I'm
just not comfortable

00:49:41.200 --> 00:49:42.350
in front of the camera.

00:49:43.314 --> 00:49:44.510
But I got through it.

00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:49.240
- Mark Laita, I'm very
cognizant of obviously Americans

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:51.050
in America and that's
why this show is called

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:54.310
Watching America, but
I'm incredibly intrigued

00:49:54.310 --> 00:49:56.820
and rewarded when I
think about the persons

00:49:56.820 --> 00:49:58.860
that make the fabric of this nation,

00:49:58.860 --> 00:50:01.480
particularly persons who do important work

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:03.550
and you do important work.

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:08.550
I am so grateful to you
for presenting Jerry to me

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:10.950
and millions of other viewers

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:15.400
and persons like Jerry, Sarah
for all of her complexity,

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:17.360
Kiara or Kelly whichever you prefer,

00:50:17.360 --> 00:50:21.330
Sweet Pea and hundreds and
hundreds of people more,

00:50:21.330 --> 00:50:25.020
you strike me as a
person who's not at ease

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:30.020
perhaps with praise or being
lauded with recognition,

00:50:30.450 --> 00:50:33.200
but I hope that you'll amend
that for the next 10 seconds

00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:36.310
as I say, that I think
you are truly an artist

00:50:36.310 --> 00:50:40.330
and your life has purpose and
meaning in it's own right,

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:41.950
but beyond that, for what you do

00:50:41.950 --> 00:50:45.010
and what you're bringing
to America's attention.

00:50:45.010 --> 00:50:46.730
We may not have an immediate kind of seer

00:50:46.730 --> 00:50:49.480
for what you portray and present to us,

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:51.930
but indeed you do awaken things in us,

00:50:51.930 --> 00:50:55.210
that otherwise should
not have gone un-awoken

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:57.560
and I want to thank you for that so much.

00:50:57.560 --> 00:50:59.560
It's been a delight talking to you Mark.

00:51:00.452 --> 00:51:01.350
- My pleasure, thank you so much.

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:03.695
- Take care sir and God bless.

00:51:03.695 --> 00:51:04.790
- Thank you Alan, bye bye.

00:51:04.790 --> 00:51:07.530
- [Woman] Watching America
is is a production of WHRV

00:51:07.530 --> 00:51:09.793
public media in Norfolk, Virginia.

